(Q: Explain the critical role of fast-responding battery energy storage in grid frequency) Tj
T* (regulation, including specific operational requirements, benefits, and quantify the current) Tj
T* (and projected market size in the U.S.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 12 Tf
13.7999999999999989 TL
0. 0.392 0. rg
56.6929133858267775 666.1419685039369369 Td
(A: Fast-responding battery energy storage is crucial for grid frequency regulation due to its) Tj
T* (rapid response time, which is significantly faster than traditional generators like gas) Tj
T* (turbines. Grid operators, such as PJM in the northeastern U.S., are required to provide) Tj
T* (regulation services \(e.g., 0.7% of their demand; 1 GW for a 140 GW peak load\). Faster) Tj
T* (resources provide more benefit for system control, leading the U.S. regulator \(FERC\) to) Tj
T* (encourage their adoption and reward improved performance. These faster resources are) Tj
T* (expected to work harder, typically having 2.5 to 3 times the charge/discharge activity) Tj
T* (\('mileage'\) of slower resources for the same nominal peak power. PJM calculates that) Tj
T* (faster regulation resources could address 40%–50% of its existing requirements, indicating) Tj
T* (an existing U.S. market size of around 2 GW for fast-responding regulation. With) Tj
T* (increasing renewable generation, the demand for frequency regulation is projected to rise) Tj
T* (from approximately 1% of capacity to 2%–7% of capacity when wind penetration reaches) Tj
T* (20%.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 14 Tf
16.0999999999999979 TL
